  "query_preview": "(This evening through Thursday)...\nIssued at 201 PM EDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nSurface analysis this afternoon shows strong high pressure in place \nover the southern Great Lakes, stretching from Lake Erie to Northern \nIL. GOES19 shows diurnal CU across central Indiana. Light E-SE \nsurface flow was in place across our forecast area. Aloft, water \nvapor shows strong ridging over the western CONUS resulting in NW \nflow from the Canadian plains spilling into the Ohio Valley. Strong \nsubsidence was shown across Indiana and Ohio. Dew point temperatures \nwere in the comfortable upper 40s to lower 50s.\n\nTonight through Saturday...\n\nDiurnal CU across the area this evening is expected to diminish as \nheating is lost, leading to clear skies overnight. Clear skies \novernight along with light winds and dew points in the 50s will lead \nto another cool night, with lows in the mid to upper 50s.\n\nOvernight convection is expected upstream over the plains states. \nModels suggest CI blowoff from this to arrive across Central Indiana \non Friday. Forecast soundings suggest high saturation through the \nday indicative of this. This will lead to another partly cloudy day \nwith slightly warmer temperatures.\n\nOverall, the persistent northwest flow aloft will remain on Friday \nand Saturday due to the strong upper ridging over the western CONUS. \nForecast soundings suggest some lower level saturation on Saturday \nas some upper forcing is expected to pass, but confidence is low in \nthese features at this point as Indiana will still be under the \ninfluence of the surface high well to the east. For now, We will \nkeep pops out of the forecast for Saturday, but we will expect more \nclouds on both Friday and Saturday due to these signals. Of note, \nwarmer and more humid air will arrive by Saturday as winds become \nmore southerly and dew points are suggested to rise into the 60s.\n\nSunday through Thursday...\n\nWarm summer weather with near normal temperatures and daily chances \nfor showers and thunderstorms.\n\nModels suggest ongoing NW flow aloft across Central Indiana during \nthis period due to a well established ridge that will be set up over \nwestern CONUS. Models differ a bit in handling placement and \nexistence of embedded short waves that look to pass through Indiana \nor points east within that flow aloft. This will keep rain chances \nconfidence low. However, several factors will remain favorable for \ndiurnal convection also.\n\nWhile one models remains unorganized within the lower levels, others \nsuggest a cold front pushing across Central Indiana on Tuesday \nattached to a low pressure system moving through the eastern Great \nLakes. Furthermore forecast soundings suggest a warm and humid \ncolumn in place each of these days with high CAPE values present and \ndew points returning the upper 60s and 70s. A cap looks to be \npresent most days, however this does not appear to be insurmountable.\n\nThus overall, confidence remains low until more of a unified signal \nappears, but enough is present to include storm chances. Should the \npotential stronger organization come into play, severe storms cannot \nbe ruled out.",
